The attached image, Figure 4 from the PDF, illustrates how Nano-11 is actively transported by antigen-presenting cells (APCs) to the draining lymph node over time. Panels A and B show that Nano-11-containing myeloid cells in the lymph node peak at 3.5% on day 2 post-injection, decreasing to 0.5% by day 7. Panel C demonstrates that migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs) consistently comprise the majority of Nano-11-positive cells (60-80%) in the lymph node across observed time points. Panel D further reveals that MigDCs exhibit significantly higher mean fluorescence intensity (MFI) of Nano-11 per cell, indicating their greater uptake capacity compared to other APC subtypes like lymphoid tissue resident DCs (LTDCs) and monocytes/macrophages (Mo/MΦ).